      "math_academy_id": "e-4146",
      "problem": "Solve for $x$ if $\\ln(4x+5)=3.$",
      "worked_solution": "To remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The logarithm is a natural logarithm, which means its base is $e.$ So, we use a base of $e$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x+5) & =3 \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x+5)} & ={e}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we know that $e^{\\ln(4x+5)} = 4x+5.$ \n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+5 & ={e}^{3} \\\\ 4x & ={e}^{3}-5 \\\\ x & =\\frac{{e}^{3}-5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
